**Answer: No.**

The hypothesis "every net converging in $(X,\mathcal{T}_2)$ also converges in $(X,\mathcal{T}_1)$" is to be read in the non-Hausdorff sense: a net that has *some* limit in $\mathcal{T}_2$ has *some* limit in $\mathcal{T}_1$ (not necessarily the same point, since limits need not be unique when $\mathcal{T}_2$ is not Hausdorff). Under this reading the implication fails, as the following counterexample shows.

**Counterexample.** Let $X=\{a,b\}$. Set
$$\mathcal{T}_2=\{\varnothing,X\}\quad\text{(indiscrete topology)},\qquad \mathcal{T}_1=\{\varnothing,\{a\},X\}\quad\text{(Sierpiński topology)}.$$

* $\mathcal{T}_2$ is not Hausdorff (it is the indiscrete topology on a two-point set).

* **Every net converges in $\mathcal{T}_2$.** In the indiscrete topology the only neighborhood of any point is $X$, so every net is eventually in every neighborhood of every point; hence every net converges to *both* $a$ and $b$ in $\mathcal{T}_2$.

* **Every net converges in $\mathcal{T}_1$.** In $\mathcal{T}_1=\{\varnothing,\{a\},X\}$ the only open neighborhood of $b$ is $X$. Therefore a net converges to $b$ iff it is eventually in $X$, which is automatically true. Hence *every* net converges (to $b$) in $\mathcal{T}_1$.

Consequently the hypothesis is satisfied: every net converging in $(X,\mathcal{T}_2)$ also converges in $(X,\mathcal{T}_1)$.

* **Yet $\mathcal{T}_1\not\subseteq\mathcal{T}_2$.** The set $\{a\}$ belongs to $\mathcal{T}_1$ but not to $\mathcal{T}_2$, so $\mathcal{T}_1$ is not a subset of $\mathcal{T}_2$.

**Why non-Hausdorffness is essential.** When $\mathcal{T}_2$ is Hausdorff, limits in $\mathcal{T}_2$ are unique, so "converges in $\mathcal{T}_2$" pins down a specific limit point $x$, and the hypothesis becomes "every net converging to $x$ in $\mathcal{T}_2$ also converges to $x$ in $\mathcal{T}_1$." The standard argument then *does* force $\mathcal{T}_1\subseteq\mathcal{T}_2$: if some $U\in\mathcal{T}_1\setminus\mathcal{T}_2$ existed, picking $x\in U$ and using the directed set of $\mathcal{T}_2$-neighborhoods of $x$ (each meeting $X\setminus U$ since $U\notin\mathcal{T}_2$) yields a net converging to $x$ in $\mathcal{T}_2$ but never entering $U$, hence not converging to $x$ in $\mathcal{T}_1$ — a contradiction. The counterexample above exploits precisely the failure of uniqueness of limits: the net "converges in $\mathcal{T}_1$" by escaping to the point $b$ whose only neighborhood is $X$, bypassing the obstruction at $a$.

Therefore the implication does **not** hold in general when $\mathcal{T}_2$ is not Hausdorff.

